## How the Approver reads this file

For every PR the approve skill is invoked on:

1. Determine PR type per **Type detection** (below).
2. Apply **Baseline criteria** (every type).
3. Apply **Test representativeness** to every added/changed test.
4. Apply the **Per-type must-have criteria** for the detected type —
   the core section first, then the language overlay's additions for
   the same type.
5. Build a risk register from the **Per-type risk factors** (core +
   overlay), folding in the **Review dossier** if the PR body carries one.
6. Calibrate confidence per **Confidence calibration** (the overlay may
   add caps).
7. Render the verdict: `APPROVE` / `REQUEST_CHANGES` / `COMMENT`-with-reservations.

If type cannot be unambiguously determined, the Approver emits an
"ambiguous type" finding and `REQUEST_CHANGES`.

**Metadata claims are verified against live state before they block.**
Before any non-`APPROVE` verdict (`REQUEST_CHANGES` or `COMMENT`) is
posted or printed (dry-run included), **every** finding grounded in PR
**metadata** — the body, title, or labels, as opposed to code findings
from the diff — is re-verified: the Approver re-fetches the live PR
object and confirms each such claim against that fresh read; an
earlier fetch may be stale or wrong. When the successful re-fetch
contradicts the earlier read, **live state governs**: the finding is
dropped or amended; when the corrected metadata feeds an earlier step
(type detection, the linked-issue lookup, a baseline criterion), that
step and everything downstream re-runs against the live values; then
the verdict is re-derived before anything is posted or printed — never
an `APPROVE` from the calibration mapping alone. A failed re-fetch is a
tool failure, never a contradiction — the finding stays (marked
unverified) and no verdict is upgraded on a failed read. A stale
metadata read must never cost a review round-trip. (The hotfix special
case is exempt — its title read is already live at post time.)

