## Review dossier (when present)

A PR opened through the autonomous review loop carries a **Review dossier** in
its body — a hidden `<!-- review-dossier: {…} -->` JSON block that records what
the pre-merge reviewer panel already found and fixed. When it is present, parse
it and fold it into the risk register (it is **context, never a rubber stamp** —
CI and the per-type criteria still decide):

- **A `clean` dimension lowers that lens's residual-risk weight.** If
  `dimensions.<lens>.clean == true`, the panel reviewed that lens (bugs,
  security, performance, code_quality, tests — plus resilience on the service
  languages and swift6_compliance on Swift; note that until #1148 lands a CLEAN
  non-core lens emits **no key at all**, so the ABSENCE of `resilience` or
  `swift6_compliance` is never itself a clean signal)
  and found nothing on the changed surface; treat a fresh risk you would
  otherwise raise in that lens as lower-probability, and do not manufacture one
  just to fill the register.
- **A non-clean dimension is where to look hardest** — the panel found something
  there; confirm the fix rather than assume it. **Unless it reports `open > 0`**
  — see the residue rule below, where "confirm the fix" is the wrong question.
- **`dimensions.<lens>.open > 0` means blockers were deliberately left open, not
  missed.** The loop has one terminal that opens a PR with blockers still
  outstanding: `CONVERGED_WITH_RESIDUE`. It fires only when the last two rounds
  found **zero Criticals** and every remaining blocker sits in a file the loop's
  own previous fix pass had just written — so what is open is the implementer
  reviewing its own last edits, not shipped behaviour going wrong. Each one was
  **filed as a labelled follow-up issue** (`review-residue` +
  `needs-refinement`), which the PR Summary names.

  Read it as **scoped, disclosed risk in that lens**, and weigh it like any other
  disclosed limitation — not as a missing fix. Do **not** `REQUEST_CHANGES`
  merely because `open > 0`: there is no fix to confirm, and demanding one asks
  for work the run deliberately deferred and recorded. Do still raise a finding
  if the diff shows the open item is **not** what the dossier says it is — a
  Critical, or a change to behaviour outside the last fix pass — because those
  are exactly the two conditions the terminal claims were checked. An older
  dossier carries no `open` key at all; absent means 0.

  **Read the Summary before extending that leniency — the dossier cannot vouch
  for the filing.** The `open` count and the words "filed as follow-up issue(s)"
  beside it are generated from the loop's **terminal alone**; the builder that
  writes them has no way to learn whether the issues were actually created and
  parented. The whole rule above rests on *tracked*, and only the Summary knows.
  So check it, and treat two shapes as **untracked** risk rather than disclosed:

  - The Summary says the remainder was **NOT filed on this run** (the PR author
    is required to say so in those words when the filing failed). Then nothing
    tracks those blockers: raise a finding naming them, and do not lower the
    lens's residual-risk weight. This is not the diff-scoped exception above —
    the open item may be exactly what the dossier says it is and still be
    untracked.
  - The Summary reconciles a **partial** filing — a count of blockers it can
    account for as **tracked** that is below `open`, or issues created but not
    parented (an unparented issue is matched by the idempotency key — label +
    exact title, resolved repo-wide — so no re-run will duplicate it *or* re-file
    it: it stays permanently orphaned and must be re-attached by hand). Weigh only the reconciled, tracked portion as
    disclosed risk, and raise a finding for the rest.

    **Count tracked, not filed-this-run.** A re-run legitimately files fewer
    issues than `open`, because the idempotency read filters out the ones an
    earlier run already filed — so a Summary whose this-run count is below `open`
    but which **names pre-existing `review-residue` issues covering the
    difference** is NOT this shape. It is the tracked case below, and treating it
    as a partial filing raises a finding against issues that exist and are
    parented.

  A Summary that names the follow-up issue numbers, or reconciles pre-existing
  ones from an earlier run against `open`, is the tracked case the leniency was
  written for. A residue PR whose Summary says **nothing** about the filing does
  not clear this bar either — the terminal promises the disclosure, so its
  absence is itself the finding.
- **`waived_low` is context, not a finding.** Those are Low suggestions the loop
  logged and deliberately did not act on; never turn a waived-Low into a
  `REQUEST_CHANGES` on its own. An item a human **promoted** and the loop then
  fixed is normally **not** in this list — it is excluded from `waived_low` and
  counted under its dimension's `blocking` instead. The exclusion requires an
  **exact** normalized title, though, so a suggestion the panel *reworded*
  between the two phases can remain listed even though it was promoted and
  fixed. So when a `promotion` object with `promoted > 0` is present, read
  `waived_low` as **conservative — it may over-list**, never as proof an item
  went un-actioned.
- **A `promotion` object means a human curated the suggestions.** When present,
  `promotion: {rounds, status, selected, promoted}` records a second, opt-in
  phase in which the human raised some waived suggestions to blocking and the
  loop cleared them: `selected` is how many they picked, `promoted` how many the
  engine actually raised. `rounds` at the top level already includes that phase.
  Read it as *extra* scrutiny the change has had, never as a defect signal — and
  note that `selected > promoted` is normal (a pick the engine never raised was
  never promoted in fact), not evidence of a missed fix.

A PR **without** a dossier (e.g. `--no-review`, or a human-authored PR) is judged
exactly as before — the dossier only ever *reduces* unnecessary friction, never
adds a gate.

