## REQUEST_CHANGES feedback loop (JSON block)

Every `REQUEST_CHANGES` verdict appends a **hidden HTML-comment JSON
block** to the review body. The `/development:maintenance` skill reads
this on the next maintenance run and dispatches the right triage agents
on the PR branch.

Schema (the `suggested_agent` vocabulary — which triage agents exist
for this language — is listed in the overlay):

```json
{
  "approver_version": "v1",
  "verdict": "REQUEST_CHANGES",
  "confidence": "LOW",
  "type_detected": "feat",
  "findings": [
    {
      "category": "test_quality | api_stability | coverage | risk | type_ambiguity | baseline | …",
      "dimension": "bugs | security | performance | code_quality | tests | resilience | swift6_compliance | … | null",
      "title": "Short headline",
      "detail": "Multi-line markdown explanation",
      "suggested_agent": "<an agent from the overlay's vocabulary> | null",
      "file": "path/to/file",
      "line": 42
    }
  ]
}
```

`dimension` is **not** a closed enum. The five core dimensions —
`bugs`, `security`, `performance`, `code_quality`, `tests` — are what
every language panel ships, but a panel may carry more: `resilience` on the
service languages, `swift6_compliance` on Swift. The
`/development-{{APPROVER_LANG}}:review` panel's own dimension table is the
authority on which (the overlay carries the `suggested_agent` vocabulary, not
the dimension set). Tag a risk with whatever dimension its lens actually
carries; never drop or mislabel a finding because its dimension is absent
from this list.

The block is wrapped in an HTML comment so it doesn't render in the
GitHub UI:

```markdown
<!-- claude-approver:findings
{ … the JSON above … }
-->
```

`/development:maintenance` parses the most recent Approver review's
hidden block, maps each finding's `suggested_agent` to the right triage
agent, dispatches them on the PR branch, pushes the fixes, and then you
re-run `/development-{{APPROVER_LANG}}:approve <PR>` once CI is green
again. There is **no automatic re-trigger** — the Approver is
user-invoked locally, not driven by a workflow.
